steps.body:
Programs make decisions all the time: show an error if the email field is empty, offer free shipping when the cart is over 50, say good morning or good evening by the time of day. Every decision is built from a question with a yes-or-no answer, and code that does one thing or another depending on it.

## Comparing values

A comparison asks a question about two values, and its answer is a boolean, `true` or `false`:

| Operator | Asks | Example | Answer |
|---|---|---|---|
| `===` | are they the same? | `5 === 5` | `true` |
| `!==` | are they different? | `5 !== 5` | `false` |
| `<` and `>` | is it smaller, or bigger? | `3 < 10` | `true` |
| `<=` and `>=` | smaller or equal, or bigger or equal? | `10 >= 10` | `true` |

`===` has three equals signs. One, `=`, assigns a value, as the previous part showed. Three compares. JavaScript also has `==`, with two, which converts the values before it compares, so `"5" == 5` is `true` even though one is a string and one is a number. That leads to bugs that are hard to find, so always use `===` and `!==`, which also check the type.

## Combining questions

Three **logical operators** combine booleans:

- `&&`, and, is `true` when both sides are `true`: `age >= 18 && hasTicket`.
- `||`, or, is `true` when at least one side is `true`: `isWeekend || isHoliday`.
- `!`, not, turns `true` into `false` and back: `!dogsAllowed`.

## if and else

An `if` statement runs some code only when a **condition**, a question like those above, is `true`:

```javascript
if (cartTotal >= 50) {
  console.log("Free shipping!");
}
```

The condition goes in round brackets. The code to run goes in curly brackets, and that group of statements is called a **block**. Its lines are indented, pushed in by two spaces, so you can see what belongs to the `if`. The browser doesn't need the indent, but people reading your code do.

`else` adds a block that runs when the condition is `false`, and `else if` adds more questions in between. The browser checks them from the top, runs the first block whose condition is `true`, and skips the rest:

```javascript
if (hour < 12) {
  console.log("Good morning");
} else if (hour < 18) {
  console.log("Good afternoon");
} else {
  console.log("Good evening");
}
```

At 9 it says good morning. At 15 the first condition is `false`, so it checks the second, which is `true`. At 20 both are `false`, so the `else` block runs. Exactly one of the three runs every time.

## Truthy and falsy

A condition doesn't have to be a boolean. Give `if` any value, and JavaScript treats it as `true` or `false`. Six values count as `false`, and they're called **falsy**:

- `false`
- `0`
- `""`, an empty string
- `null` and `undefined`
- `NaN`

Everything else is **truthy**, which counts as `true`, including any string with something in it and any number that isn't zero. That gives a short way to ask whether there's anything there at all:

```javascript
if (email) {
  console.log("Thanks!");
} else {
  console.log("Please enter your email.");
}
```

If `email` is an empty string, the field was left blank, and the `else` block runs. You'll write checks like this whenever you read what someone typed. Watch out for 0, though: an amount of 0 is falsy, so `if (amount)` treats zero as missing. When zero is a real answer, compare it directly, as in `amount === 0`.

examples.code:
const temperature = 31;

if (temperature >= 30) {
  console.log("Hot: bring extra water.");
} else if (temperature >= 15) {
  console.log("Mild: a good day for a walk.");
} else {
  console.log("Cold: bring a jacket.");
}

console.log(5 === 5);
console.log("5" === 5);
console.log("5" == 5);
console.log(3 !== 4);
console.log(10 > 3 && 2 > 5);
console.log(10 > 3 || 2 > 5);
console.log(!true);

const searchText = "";

if (searchText) {
  console.log(`Searching for ${searchText}`);
} else {
  console.log("Type something to search.");
}

const itemsInCart = 0;

if (itemsInCart) {
  console.log(`${itemsInCart} items in your cart`);
} else {
  console.log("Your cart is empty.");
}

steps.body:
Programs do the same jobs again and again: work out the shipping on every order, format every price, update a counter every time someone clicks. A **function** is a set of steps you write once, give a name, and run whenever you need it. Functions are also how a page responds to people: you hand the browser a function, and it runs it when something happens, like a click.

## Making and calling a function

```javascript
function shippingCost(total) {
  if (total >= 50) {
    return 0;
  }
  return 5;
}
```

The keyword `function` starts it, then comes its name, `shippingCost`, and in round brackets its **parameters**: names for the values it needs, here `total`. The block in curly brackets is the **body**, the steps it runs.

Writing a function doesn't run it. To run it, you **call** it: its name, then round brackets holding the values to use, called **arguments**:

```javascript
shippingCost(42);
```

During this call, `total` is 42. The body runs, the condition is `false`, and `return 5` hands 5 back to whoever called it. That's the **return value**, and the call itself stands for it. So you can store it, as in `const shipping = shippingCost(42)`, or use it in a sum, as in `42 + shippingCost(42)`.

```diagram javascript-basics-function-call
A call hands its arguments to the function's parameters, runs the body, and gets the return value back.
```

In the diagram, step 1 is the call, which hands 42 to the parameter `total`. Step 2 is the body running with that value. In step 3, `return` sends 5 back, and the call `shippingCost(42)` is replaced by 5 where it was written.

`return` also ends the function at once, so in the example, a total of 80 returns 0 and never reaches `return 5`. A function with no `return` gives back `undefined`, which is fine for functions that do something, like print, rather than work something out.

## Names inside stay inside

Variables made inside a function's body, with `const` or `let`, exist only while it runs, and only inside it. Code outside can't see them. The same goes for any block, like an `if`'s. That's called **scope**, and it's a good thing: each function can use simple names like `total` or `message` without clashing with the same names elsewhere. When a function needs to give something back, it returns it.

## Arrow functions

There's a shorter way to write a function, called an **arrow function**, after its `=>`:

```javascript
const double = (n) => n * 2;
```

The parameters go in brackets, then `=>`, then what the function returns. `double(21)` is 42. When the body needs more than one line, use curly brackets and a `return`, the same as before:

```javascript
const shippingCost = (total) => {
  if (total >= 50) {
    return 0;
  }
  return 5;
};
```

Both kinds do the same job here. You'll see arrow functions most when you hand a function to someone else, which comes next.

## Handing a function to the browser

A function is a value, like a number or a string, so you can store it in a variable or hand it to another function as an argument. A function you hand over to be called later is a **callback**.

The browser has functions that take callbacks. `setTimeout` takes a function and a number of milliseconds, thousandths of a second, and calls your function once that time has passed:

```javascript
setTimeout(() => {
  console.log("One second later!");
}, 1000);
```

The arrow function here has no parameters, so its brackets are empty. `setTimeout` doesn't wait: it notes the function and returns at once, and the script carries on with its next line. A second later, the browser calls the function. That's the pattern behind every button and every form in the next lesson: you give the browser a function, and it calls it when someone clicks.

examples.code:
function shippingCost(total) {
  if (total >= 50) {
    return 0;
  }
  return 5;
}

console.log(shippingCost(42));
console.log(shippingCost(80));

const cart = 42;
console.log(`To pay: ${cart + shippingCost(cart)}`);

const double = (n) => n * 2;
console.log(double(21));

console.log("Asking the browser to wait one second...");
setTimeout(() => {
  console.log("One second later!");
}, 1000);
console.log("This line runs before the timer.");

function greet(visitor) {
  const message = `Welcome, ${visitor}!`;
  return message;
}

console.log(greet("Ana"));
console.log(message);

const trail = {
  name: "Lake Loop",
  lengthKm: 5,
  dogsAllowed: true,
};

console.log(trail.name);
console.log(trail["lengthKm"]);

trail.lengthKm = 5.5;
trail.difficulty = "easy";
console.log(`${trail.name}: ${trail.lengthKm} km, ${trail.difficulty}`);
console.log(trail.elevation);

const trailName = "Lake Loop";

console.log(trailName.length);
console.log(trailName.toUpperCase());
console.log(trailName.includes("Lake"));
console.log(Math.round(5.6));
console.log(typeof console, typeof console.log);

const trail = { name: "Lake Loop" };

console.log(trail.start);
console.log(trail.start.lat);

const trails = ["Lake Loop", "Ridge Trail", "North Loop"];

console.log(trails[0]);
console.log(trails[2]);
console.log(trails.length);
console.log(trails[trails.length - 1]);
console.log(trails[3]);

trails.push("Cliff Path");
console.log(trails.length, trails.includes("Cliff Path"));

const cart = [
  { name: "Water bottle", price: 12 },
  { name: "Trail map", price: 6 },
  { name: "Snack bar", price: 2 },
];

let total = 0;

for (const item of cart) {
  console.log(`${item.name}: ${item.price}`);
  total += item.price;
}

console.log(`Total: ${total}`);

const trails = [
  { name: "Lake Loop", lengthKm: 5, dogsAllowed: true },
  { name: "Ridge Trail", lengthKm: 12, dogsAllowed: false },
  { name: "North Loop", lengthKm: 8, dogsAllowed: true },
];

const dogFriendly = trails.filter((trail) => trail.dogsAllowed);
const names = dogFriendly.map((trail) => trail.name);
console.log(names.join(", "));

const short = trails.find((trail) => trail.lengthKm < 6);
console.log(short.name);
